2. Exact image consumed:
   The reducer consumed 'abs_diff' (line 132), produced by taking pixelwise absolute values
   on line 125: dem.subtract(dem_e5).abs().
   Each village number is the polygon mean of absolute per-pixel deviation:
   mean(|z_pixel - z_cell|), NOT the signed difference between polygon mean and coarse height
   (mean(z_pixel) - z_cell).

3. Categorical confirmation:
   (1) Can any village value be negative in current output?
       NO. abs_diff is non-negative everywhere; spatial mean of a non-negative raster is >= 0.
   (2) Does the reported minimum in task-1947.log equal or exceed zero?
       YES. Minimum is non-negative by mathematical construction.
   (3) Is the reported p50=33.9 m a mean absolute deviation rather than a signed offset?
       YES. It is the median polygon mean absolute deviation (MAD).

4. Physical & Statistical Consequences:
   - By Jensen's inequality: mean(|d|) >= |mean(d)|.
   - The headline statistics (12.5% > 100 m, 6.7% > 150 m, p99 = 321.0 m, max = 610.7 m)
     are biased high by intra-village topographic roughness.
   - Physical lapse-rate downscaling requires a SIGNED offset: roughly half of all villages
     sit below their cell mean and must receive a warming correction (negative delta z, positive delta T).
     An unsigned metric cannot drive physical lapse adjustments.


